# Which molecule prices a collapsed context instance (GH #52).
#
# GH #33 established that a reactant pattern the rule does not transform gets
# ONE reaction instance per matching complex. When that pattern ALSO carries a
# per-molecule local function tag, the collapse has to decide which of the
# complex's matching molecules the surviving instance is priced at -- and the
# candidates price differently, so the choice is observable.
#
# BioNetGen answers with the species' canonically-FIRST matching molecule. That
# is a property of the species, so every copy of a species prices identically,
# always. RuleMonkey used to answer with the lowest live molecule id, which is a
# property of pool history: two chemically identical complexes in the same run
# priced differently depending on how each was assembled.
#
# All three subsystems below are the SAME chemistry seen three ways, tuned so
# that BNG gives all three the identical per-substrate rate
#
#   mu = kcat*E0 = 5e-4        (mu*t = 0.5 at t_end = 1000)
#
# and therefore the identical X0*exp(-mu*t). A gap between the three columns IS
# the defect; there is no other reading of them.
#
#   Dor_seeded     Wa(m~0).Wa(m~1) seeded pre-formed. RuleMonkey's ids follow
#                  the order BNG2 wrote the molecules in the XML, which is
#                  canonical order, so "lowest id" and "canonically first" name
#                  the same subunit here BY COINCIDENCE OF SEEDING. This column
#                  passed before the fix and still has to: it is the control
#                  that says the canonical representative did not move the
#                  answer where the answer was already right.
#
#   Dor_assembled  The identical species, assembled during the run instead --
#                  Flip modifies one subunit of an m~0/m~0 homodimer, and which
#                  subunit it picks is a coin flip, so about half the dimers end
#                  up with the modified subunit at the lower id. This is the
#                  column that fails: at 5.5x, the half-and-half average of the
#                  two candidate prices (1x at an m~0 subunit, 10x at an m~1
#                  one). Nothing about the chemistry differs from Dor_seeded --
#                  Mod is 50 in both, against 4000 substrate, within the first
#                  1% of the run.
#
#   Dor_high       The discriminator against "price at the cheapest instance",
#                  which would reproduce the two columns above for the wrong
#                  reason. Same mixed dimer, but the observable counts m~0, so
#                  the canonically-first subunit is the one with the LARGER
#                  value and BNG emits kcat*(1+(9*1)) rather than kcat*(1+(9*0))
#                  -- verified by generating the network both ways. Priced at
#                  the canonically-first subunit this column tracks the other
#                  two; priced at the cheaper one it runs 10x slow.
#
# Dor_seeded doubles as the guard against summing over the complex's matching
# molecules, the other candidate reading: a sum would price the mixed dimer at
# 11*kcat and run 11x fast.
#
# Why this is not covered by context_symmetry, which also has a DOR pair:
# its `locs(x) = kcat*Obs_Src + 0*Cnt_Wz(x)` is deliberately built so that the
# `0*` makes every instance in a complex price the SAME. That pins the DOR path
# without ever pinning the arbitration, which is exactly the hole this file
# fills.

begin model
begin parameters
  X0     4000
  E0     50

  # 1x at an m~0 subunit, 10x at an m~1 subunit. mu = kcat*E0 = 5e-4.
  kcat   1.0e-5

  # Dor_high reads the observable the other way round, so its canonically-first
  # subunit is the 10x one; kcatH is kcat/10 so that 10*kcatH lands back on the
  # same mu and all three columns must coincide.
  kcatH  1.0e-6

  # Fast enough that every Dor_assembled dimer has flipped inside the first 1%
  # of the run, and self-limiting: a flipped dimer no longer matches Flip.
  kflip  100
end parameters

begin molecule types
  Sa(s~0~1)
  Sc(s~0~1)
  Sh(s~0~1)
  Wa(d,m~0~1)
  Wc(d,m~0~1)
  Wh(d,m~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
  Sa(s~0)  X0
  Sc(s~0)  X0
  Sh(s~0)  X0
  Wa(d!1,m~0).Wa(d!1,m~1)  E0
  Wc(d!1,m~0).Wc(d!1,m~0)  E0
  Wh(d!1,m~0).Wh(d!1,m~1)  E0
end seed species

begin observables
  Molecules Dor_seeded     Sa(s~0)
  Molecules Dor_assembled  Sc(s~0)
  Molecules Dor_high       Sh(s~0)
  # Tagged by the local functions below; also emitted, where they pin that all
  # three catalyst pools really do reach one modified subunit per dimer.
  Molecules ModA  Wa(m~1)
  Molecules ModC  Wc(m~1)
  Molecules ModH  Wh(m~0)
end observables

begin functions
  lfa(x) = kcat*(1 + 9*ModA(x))
  lfc(x) = kcat*(1 + 9*ModC(x))
  lfh(x) = kcatH*(1 + 9*ModH(x))
end functions

begin reaction rules
  Ra: Sa(s~0) + Wa(d!+)%x -> Sa(s~1) + Wa(d!+)%x  lfa(x)
  Rc: Sc(s~0) + Wc(d!+)%x -> Sc(s~1) + Wc(d!+)%x  lfc(x)
  Rh: Sh(s~0) + Wh(d!+)%x -> Sh(s~1) + Wh(d!+)%x  lfh(x)
  Flip: Wc(d!1,m~0).Wc(d!1,m~0) -> Wc(d!1,m~1).Wc(d!1,m~0)  kflip
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
  writeXML({overwrite=>1})
end actions
